ngoing processes in a local high technology company. Priority enrollment given to engineering majors.","pre":null,"unl":["ENG 207"],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"ENG 203":{"t":"Applied Innovations","u":"4","desc":"Course includes the examination of business plans developed by early stage technology businesses. Students expected to work on the development of business plans for real, innovative business organizations. Will explore all of the business research and analysis that needs to be undertaken in order to develop a complete business plan. Completion of ENG 201 or ENG 202 preferred.","pre":null,"unl":["ENG 207"],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"ENG 207":{"t":"Corporate Entrepreneurship for Global Competitiveness","u":"4","desc":"Explore corporate entrepreneurship and innovation process using the medical device industry as a case example. This highly dynamic industry segment provides an approachable model to analyze corporate strategies and behaviors that affect overall competitiveness.","pre":"ENG 201, ENG 202, ENG 203, or consent of the instructor.","unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"ENG 208":{"t":"Basics of Entrepreneurism and Technology Commercialization","u":"4","desc":"Learn basics of start-up through a series of lectures, workshops, and assignments. Students will learn to effectively communicate the values of their ideas to investors and partners. Learn to validate market potential of a technology.","pre":"graduate standing.","unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"ENG 210":{"t":"Systems Engineering","u":"4","desc":"This course examines the complexity of integrating components of product engineering, marketing and client requirements into a single engineering system. Case studies explore successes and failures in engineered systems and how to detect and avoid potential failures using strong leadership skills, systemic analysis and thinking.","pre":"graduate standing.","unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"ENG 211":{"t":"Engineering Leadership in Academia","u":"4","desc":"This course is designed to teach leadership in teaching and learning at the college/university level. Students will learn through readings on engineering leadership, proposal development, ethics, and cognitive science, and thorough practical experience writing and developing course and project development plans.","pre":"graduate standing.","unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"ENG 30":{"t":"Creative Problem-Solving for the Twenty-First-Century Changemaker","u":"4","desc":"Students learn problem-solving strategies and heuristics and apply them in lab modules and a quarter-long team project. Students will employ strategies to identify problems, test and verify assumptions, and develop solutions. Strategies include Duncker Diagram, fishbone diagram, systems thinking, KT analysis, and Five Whys.","pre":null,"unl":[],"hist":[["SP26",0,3.69,69.5,17.3,4.3,0,0,4.3,4.3,0],["SP25",1,3.39,52.6,26.3,15.7,0,0,0,5.3,0]],"ge":["muir:Social Sciences","erc:Quantitative Methods","seventh:Alternatives · Nat Sci & Eng","eighth:Breadth · Quant & Eng"],"gpa":3.537,"aRate":61,"terms":2,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[{"i":"Rob Wolfram Schlom","g":3.686,"A":69.5,"B":17.3,"C":4.34,"D":0,"F":0,"W":4.34,"P":4.34,"NP":0,"n":1,"y":26,"cur":0,"rq":null,"rd":null,"rw":null,"rn":null,"rid":null},{"i":"Curt Schurgers","g":3.389,"A":52.6,"B":26.3,"C":15.7